Ordering something wasn't always the hard part.

100
00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:26.240
Getting it home was.

101
00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.520
This logistics history examines the missing last mile.

102
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:36.240
When packages could reach a railway station, but customers still had to collect them themselves.

103
00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.160
Arrived at the train station.

104
00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:40.720
You didn't get a text saying your parcel has arrived.

105
00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:41.600
Please come pick up.

106
00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.400
You gotta check in.

107
00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:43.520
Have your neighbors check in.

108
00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.200
Yeah, you gotta keep going there every day, yeah.

109
00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.720
Wait, Mark, that last segment of delivery, is that what we would call last mile delivery nowadays?

110
00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:51.680
Yes.

111
00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.080
Uh and last mile delivery generally didn't exist.

112
00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:59.280
You could get your package to a train station.

113
00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:05.600
Railway Express, what became Railway Express did that, and the express companies did that using trains.

114
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:10.240
But getting it from there to your house was another issue.

115
00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.840
And remember, this was an overwhelmingly rural country.

116
00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.480
Most people lived uh on farms or in in small towns away from the trains.

117
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.080
And so this was a challenge.

118
00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.400
And there was a lot of resentment about this.

119
00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:29.040
Uh farm groups had been pushing for the government to start carrying parcels for 20 years at that point in time.

120
00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.120
And they had not had any success.

121
00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:37.680
So it was not until uh 1912 that Congress finally agreed uh to establish a parcel post.

122
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:49.440
Okay, so that service exists, and reading in your book, it was really fascinating to hear how it kind of gives rise to the era of the Sears and Macy's catalogs and all those good things.

123
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:57.680
What were the downsides or what was still lacking such that there was a market available for a private parcel delivery service?

124
00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:58.640
Sure.

125
00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:09.080
The catalogs hadn't existed before the parcel post came along, but their business was much smaller because they too could only send their stuff as far as a railway station.

126
00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:11.160
And customers had to pick it up.

127
00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.960
So they their business really exploded after the parcel post started.

128
00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.480
There were delivery issues in the cities, too.

129
00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:30.200
You know, a lot of the big department store chains were excuse me, you know, the big department stores were established in the early 20th century or the late 19th century.

130
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:35.320
And one of the things they offered to their customers was delivery.

131
00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:41.560
These stores were very large, very elaborate.
